/P 75 0 R
/E (bifurcated digital society: individuals who comprehend AI and can navigate its implications, vs those who are only subjugated to automated decisions. This necessitates the allocation of resources towards citizen recourse mechanisms public legal assistance, and accessible complaint channels. This also entails assisting small and medium enterprises to ensure that responsible AI does not become a privilege exclusive to major firms. Policy may also promote "ethical procurement" inside the public sector: governments ought to mandate that vendors supply auditability, transparency documentation, explicit accountability. The establishment of elevated requirements by the public sector generates market incentives for reliable AI. Thus, regulation serves not merely as a limitation but as a catalyst for enhanced innovation.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
