/P 75 0 R
/E (AI governance must not to depend solely on the individual integrity. Public policy is essential since of AI influences various aspects of public life, and including labor markets, media ecosystems, security, education, and health. Ethics must consequently be established as infrastructure encompa -ssing legislation, standards, oversight, public responsibility. UNESCO \(2021\) underscores the necessity of human rights based methodologies; the European Commission \(2019\) accentuates the importance of reliable AI. These frameworks are beneficial; yet they require implementation competence.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
224 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 75 0 R
/E (Policy must also provide resources towards education and cultural development. In the absence of a technologically educated society, regulation is feeble and manipulation flourishes. Similarly, public entities must exemplify ethical AI implementation. Governments should refrain from the inclination to employ AI for clandestine spying. or political domination, as this would expedite community distrust and the deterioration of democracy. Furthermore, policy should promote plural governance through partnership among academia, civic society, industry, and government. Ethical artificial intelligence necessitates societal discourse. In the absence of communication, regulation may devolve into a technical framework or become influenced by the vested interests. In conclusion, public policy must prioritize the safeguarding of dignity, equity, and democratic integrity while facilitating innovation. The objective is not to hinder advancement but to guarantee that improvement benefits living. In Europe, where human rights and social models are paramount,  and a  significant  policy  problem  is  to avert  a )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
